<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khujand</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Leninabad, Khodjent</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khujand is the second city of Tajikistan, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> at the entrance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Fergana Valley</a>. One of the oldest towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, it is identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria-eschate">Alexandria Eschate</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> in 329 BCE, and was a station on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>. Known as Leninabad from 1936 to 1991, its region supplied much of the Soviet-era Tajik ruling elite.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khujand.jpg" alt="Khujand" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User:EH · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_to_Khujand.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
